          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Unit 1 capstone \u2014 A reproducible climate-science argument",
          "content": "Start from the research question **How can a climate trend be distinguished from weather noise, instrument shifts and a changing reference period?**. Create an evidence map that distinguishes the three sources **station quality-controlled time series**, **independent neighboring climate observations** and **baseline sensitivity analysis**. The first source should establish the main physical state, the second should show whether the proposed forcing was present, and the third should offer a genuinely independent check. For the synthetic scenario, state a primary hypothesis and an alternative that differs in mechanism rather than merely in parameter size. Specify what both would predict for the same geographic area under different observing or forcing conditions. In your technical report, use no unexplained uncertainty bars; distinguish calibration limits, observation support, model approximation, initialization errors, and uncertainty about the process itself.\n\n**Data and reproducibility task:** Read the 30 synthetic records for this unit. Interpret the annual year column as a time axis, calculate a decadal mean and robust spread, and repeat the trend after withholding every fifth year. If the trend changes materially, explain whether the underlying signal might be under-sampled. Prepare a plain-language summary of what the dataset can and cannot support. Supply enough detail that another student can reproduce every calculation without contacting the author. Deliver a transparent station-quality report before offering a trend estimate.\n\n**Critical discussion:** Suppose an institution must make a decision from the available analysis. Identify the evidence that is missing, the consequence of a false alarm versus a missed event, and the observation that would most efficiently reduce consequential uncertainty. Provide a direct recommendation only when the dataset and problem definition warrant one. Never treat synthetic climatological examples as an operational alert or regulatory threshold.",
