          "secNumber": "8.1",
          "heading": "The central climate-science investigation",
          "content": "**Question:** How can climate evidence improve decisions under uncertainty, inequality and irreversibility?\n\n**Case setting:** An imaginary coastal city compares flood barriers, early warnings, emissions reductions and managed retreat. All locations and observations in the following investigation are synthetic. The purpose is to learn which inference would survive contact with independent measurements, rather than to produce a plausible-sounding story from an incomplete climate map.\n\nA correct climate calculation does not automatically yield a preferred policy. Investment horizons, maintenance, distributional consequences, risk tolerance, discount rates and governance affect outcomes. Mitigation reduces forcing agents; adaptation changes sensitivity and exposure. Their effects should not be casually summed because they operate on different parts of a causal chain and at different time scales.\n\nRobust decisions can preserve flexibility when future climate and socioeconomic outcomes remain uncertain. A false precision claim can be more harmful than a meaningful range. Monitoring and decision triggers make a project adaptive rather than permanently tied to the forecast available at initial approval. Fictional values in a classroom cost\u2013benefit model must not be presented as market or national data.\n\n### Working boundaries and research assumptions\n\nThe effective observational transect for this unit runs from **emissions policy and adaptation to decision monitoring**. Different forms of evidence support different kinds of inference: physical climate evidence constrains one aspect, transparent consequence/cost accounting another, and community and institutional monitoring a third. These are not interchangeable. A flux through a boundary differs from stored heat or carbon; a baseline-dependent anomaly is not an absolute state; a correlation alone cannot establish forcing attribution. Choose a geographic domain or explicit accounting boundary before writing the governing relationship. Record the averaging period and the geographical support used in comparisons.\n\nThe capstone compares three hypothetical policy portfolios and specifies which indicators would trigger a revision, what knowledge gaps remain and which affected groups should review the decision.\n\n### What success looks like\n\nA strong unit report reconstructs a physical chain from forcing to response while maintaining the distinction between an observation, a calculation, a model hypothesis and a decision. Include a figure that cannot be understood without axis units, a written alternative explanation and a comparison with a second type of evidence. Present a decision matrix with costs, benefits, uncertainty and revision triggers.",
